Prove that for all positive integers \(n\) and elements \(r\) of a ring, \( r^{-n} = (r^{-1})^n \).
[Proof]
We will prove this by induction on \(n\).
- Base Case: If \(n = 0\), then \(r^{-0} = r^0 = 1 = (r^{-1})^0\).
- Inductive Step: Assume that for some \(n \ge 0\), \(r^{-n} = (r^{-1})^n\). We must show that \(r^{-(n+1)} = (r^{-1})^{n+1}\). We have
$$\begin{align}
r^{-(n+1)} 
&= r^{-n} \cdot r^{-1} 
\qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \text{(by definition of $r^{-(n+1)}$)}
\\
&= (r^{-1})^n \cdot r^{-1} 
\qquad \qquad \qquad \text{(by the inductive hypothesis)}
\\
&= (r^{-1})^{n+1}.
\qquad \qquad \qquad \text{(by definition of $r^{-(n+1)}$)}
\end{align}$$

Therefore, by the Principle of Mathematical Induction, for all positive integers \(n\), \( r^{-n} = (r^{-1})^n \).

