You are provided with a theorem and its informal statement and proof:

If $ab=1$, show that $a^nb^n=1$ for all $n$.

By induction on $n$. The base case $n=0$ is trivial. For the inductive step, we have  \begin{align*}
a^{n+1}b^{n+1} & = a^n a b^n b \\
& = a^n (ab) b^n \\
& = a^n (1) b^n \\
& = a^n b^n \\
& = 1.
\end{align*}

