This follows directly from the definition of a group, which requires that the operation (in this case, multiplication) be associative and have an identity element.  Given elements a and b in a group, the inverse of their product is given by

```
(a * b)^-1 = c
```

where c is an element of the group such that

```
c * (a * b) = (a * b) * c = e
```

where e is the identity element of the group.  Multiplying both sides of the first equation by b^-1 on the left and a^-1 on the right, we get

```
b^-1 * c * (a * b) = b^-1 * (a * b) * c = b^-1 * e * a^-1
```

Simplifying, we get

```
(b^-1 * c) * a = a * (b^-1 * c) = b^-1 * a^-1
```

Therefore, b^-1 * c is the inverse of a * b, so

```
(a * b)^-1 = b^-1 * a^-1
```

as claimed.

