**Question:**

Prove that for any index $i$ in a set $I$, if $f(i)$ is a semilattice with supremum operation $\sqcup$ and neutral element $1$, then $f(i) \times f(i)$ is also a semilattice with supremum operation $(x, y) \mapsto (x \sqcup y, y \sqcup x)$ and neutral element $(1, 1)$.

**Proof:**

Let $x, y \in f(i)$. Then,

```
(x ⊔ y, y ⊔ x) = (x, y) \sqcup (y, x)
              = (x, y) ⊔ (y, x)  [by definition of $\sqcup$]
              = (x ⊔ y, y ⊔ x)  [by definition of $\sqcup$]
```

Therefore, $(x ⊔ y, y ⊔ x)$ is the supremum of $(x, y)$ and $(y, x)$ in $f(i) \times f(i)$.

Next, let $z \in f(i) \times f(i)$. Then,

```
(x ⊔ y, y ⊔ x) ⊔ z = (x, y) ⊔ z
                    = (x ⊔ z, y ⊔ z)  [by definition of $\sqcup$]
                    = (x ⊔ y, y ⊔ x) ⊔ z  [by definition of $\sqcup$]
```

Therefore, $(x ⊔ y, y ⊔ x)$ is the supremum of $(x, y)$ and $z$ in $f(i) \times f(i)$.

Finally, we have

```
(x ⊔ y, y ⊔ x) ⊔ (1, 1) = (x, y) ⊔ (1, 1)
                         = (x, y)  [by definition of $1$]
                         = (1, 1) ⊔ (x, y)  [by definition of $1$]
                         = (x ⊔ y, y ⊔ x) ⊔ (1, 1)  [by definition of $\sqcup$]
```

Therefore, $(1, 1)$ is the neutral element of $f(i) \times f(i)$.

Hence, $f(i) \times f(i)$ is a semilattice with supremum operation $(x, y) \mapsto (x \sqcup y, y \sqcup x)$ and neutral element $(1, 1)$.

