The proof proceeds by induction on n:
- Base case: n = 0. In this case, n • a = 0 • a = a by the definition of 0 • a. On the other hand, a * n = a * 0 = 0 by the definition of 0. Thus, n • a = a * n holds for n = 0.
- Inductive step: Suppose the claim holds for some n ≥ 0, i.e., n • a = a * n. We need to show that it also holds for n + 1, i.e., (n + 1) • a = a * (n + 1). By the definition of (n + 1) • a, we have
```
(n + 1) • a = n • a + a
```
By the induction hypothesis, we know that n • a = a * n. Substituting this into the above equation, we get
```
(n + 1) • a = a * n + a
```
On the other hand, we have
```
a * (n + 1) = a * n + a
```
Therefore, (n + 1) • a = a * (n + 1), which completes the induction step.
Thus, by the principle of mathematical induction, the claim holds for all n ∈ N.

