State the theorem that shows that the unop function is the identity on natural numbers.
Show that it is true.

The unop function is the identity on natural numbers. That is, for any natural number n, unop(n) = n.

This is because the unop function simply converts a natural number to its corresponding machine integer, and then back to a natural number. Since the machine integer representation of a natural number is the same as the natural number itself, the unop function has no effect on natural numbers.

To show that this is true, we can use the following proof:

```
theorem unop_natCast [NatCast α] (n : ℕ) : unop (n : αᵐᵒᵖ) = n :=
  rfl
```

In this proof, we use the rfl tactic to show that the unop function is the identity on natural numbers. The rfl tactic is a tactic that generates a proof by reflexivity, which is a logical rule that states that any expression is equal to itself. In this case, the rfl tactic generates a proof that the unop function is equal to the identity function on natural numbers.

